## Predicting functional motions with Normal Mode Analysis

```{r}
# Read in the Adenylate Kinase (ADK) structure
# ADK is an enzyme that transfers phosphate groups between nucleotides
# It undergoes large conformational changes (shape changes) when it binds substrates
adk <- read.pdb("6s36")
adk
```

```{r}
# Normal Mode Analysis (NMA) is a computational method to predict
# the natural "breathing" motions of a protein based on its structure
# It treats the protein like a network of springs connecting atoms
# The lowest frequency modes correspond to the largest scale motions
# which are often the biologically relevant conformational changes
m <- nma(adk)

# This plot shows the flexibility of each residue
# Peaks = flexible regions, valleys = rigid regions
plot(m)
```

```{r}
# mktrj() generates a "trajectory" file showing the protein moving
# along the predicted normal mode - like a movie of the motion
# We can open this file in Mol* to visualize how the protein moves
mktrj(m, file="adk_m7.pdb")
```

## 5. Comparative Structure Analysis of Adenylate Kinase

**Q10 Answer:** The `msa` package is found only on BioConductor and not CRAN. BioConductor specializes in bioinformatics packages.

**Q11 Answer:** `bio3dview` is not found on BioConductor or CRAN — it is only available on GitHub and must be installed using remotes::install_github().

**Q12 Answer:** FALSE. The `pak` package can install from GitHub, but the question refers specifically to the `devtools` package functions (devtools::install_github() and devtools::install_bitbucket()).

```{r}
# get.seq() fetches a protein sequence from the PDB database
# Here we get chain A of adenylate kinase structure 1AKE
# We will use this sequence to search for similar structures using BLAST
aa <- get.seq("1ake_A")
aa
```

**Q13 Answer:** This sequence is 214 amino acids long.

```{r}
# Instead of running BLAST (which can time out with many users),
# we use a pre-defined list of related ADK structures identified by BLAST
# These are all adenylate kinase structures from various bacterial species
hits <- NULL
hits$pdb.id <- c('1AKE_A','6S36_A','6RZE_A','3HPR_A','1E4V_A',
                 '5EJE_A','1E4Y_A','3X2S_A','6HAP_A','6HAM_A',
                 '4K46_A','3GMT_A','4PZL_A')

# get.pdb() downloads all 13 related PDB files at once
# split=TRUE splits each file into individual chains
# gzip=TRUE compresses the files to save disk space
files <- get.pdb(hits$pdb.id, path="pdbs", split=TRUE, gzip=TRUE)
```

```{r}
# pdbaln() does two things:
# 1. Aligns the sequences of all structures so equivalent residues match up
# 2. Superimposes (fits) the 3D structures on top of each other
# This is necessary before PCA so we are comparing the same positions
# across all structures
pdbs <- pdbaln(files, fit = TRUE, exefile="msa")
```

```{r}
# pdb.annotate() looks up metadata about each structure in our collection
# Here we use it to find out which species each structure came from
# This will help us interpret the PCA results later
ids <- basename.pdb(pdbs$id)
anno <- pdb.annotate(ids)
unique(anno$source)
# Note: most structures are from E. coli, but a few are from other bacteria
# This makes sense as ADK is a highly conserved enzyme found in all living things
```

```{r}
# PCA on protein structures works similarly to PCA on any other data
# Instead of gene expression values or candy properties, our "variables"
# are the x,y,z coordinates of each atom across all structures
# PC1 will capture the direction of greatest structural variation
# which often corresponds to the main functional motion of the protein
pc.xray <- pca(pdbs)

# This plot shows how much variance each PC explains
# A big drop after PC1 means most variation is captured in one motion
plot(pc.xray)
```

```{r}
# RMSD = Root Mean Square Deviation
# It measures how different two structures are from each other
# Small RMSD = similar structures, large RMSD = different structures
# We calculate ALL pairwise RMSDs to see which structures are most similar
rd <- rmsd(pdbs)

# Hierarchical clustering groups similar structures together
# cutree() cuts the resulting tree into 3 groups (k=3)
hc.rd <- hclust(dist(rd))
grps.rd <- cutree(hc.rd, k=3)

# Color the PCA plot by cluster to see which structures group together
# Structures in the same cluster have similar overall conformations
plot(pc.xray, 1:2, col="grey50", bg=grps.rd, pch=21, cex=1)
```

```{r}
# mktrj() generates a trajectory along PC1 - the direction of most variation
# This shows us the main conformational change across all ADK structures
# We can open pc_1.pdb in Mol* to watch the protein "move" between states
pc1 <- mktrj(pc.xray, pc=1, file="pc_1.pdb")

# Make a nicer labeled version of the PCA plot with ggplot2
library(ggplot2)
library(ggrepel)

df <- data.frame(PC1=pc.xray$z[,1], 
                 PC2=pc.xray$z[,2], 
                 col=as.factor(grps.rd),
                 ids=ids)

p <- ggplot(df) + 
  aes(PC1, PC2, col=col, label=ids) +
  geom_point(size=2) +
  geom_text_repel(max.overlaps = 20) +
  theme(legend.position = "none")
p
# Each dot is one ADK structure - dots close together have similar conformations
# The spread along PC1 represents the open-to-closed conformational change
# that ADK undergoes when it binds its substrate
```

## 6. Normal Mode Analysis of all ADK structures

```{r}
# Running NMA on the whole collection of structures (not just one)
# lets us compare flexibility profiles across different ADK conformations
# This tells us if different structures predict different patterns of motion
modes <- nma(pdbs)

# Plot flexibility profiles for all structures overlaid
# Black line = average, colored lines = individual structures
plot(modes, pdbs, col=grps.rd)
```

**Q14 Answer:** The black (average) and colored (individual structure) lines show both similar and different patterns. They are most similar in the rigid core regions of the protein. They differ most in two regions: the LID domain (around residues 120-160) and the AMPbind domain (around residues 30-60). These are exactly the regions that move when ADK binds its substrates — they close down around the nucleotides like a clamp. The fact that different crystal structures show different flexibility in these regions makes sense because some structures are caught in the "open" state and others in the "closed" state.

```{r}
# Always good practice to end with sessionInfo() which records
# exactly which versions of R and all packages were used
# This makes your analysis reproducible by others
sessionInfo()
```
